A question to everyone here: why do you play NetHack?  Particularly, why 
do you play it over servers (like nethack.alt.org)?

Full disclosure here - I'm asking this in part to gather material for a 
college class (Social Issues in Interactive Media and Games).  But I 
also just plain like a good story, and obviously folks here have plenty 
of those - over the past few weeks I've already seen and/or found some 
neat ones.  Yes, Father Naughty is going to be in the paper, as a 
shining example of how the experts here have gone to extreme lengths for 
an amusing (and verifiably true) story to share with the community.

I had been playing the stand-alone version of NetHack on and off for 
years, but I'd never made it terribly far (never even made it to the 
quest) - until I came here, and started playing (and occasionally 
watching) games on the NAO server.  In the process, I've found a lot of 
interesting elements in this community that I'd like to comment on in to 
my paper.  Everyone's got gameplay-enhancing(?) ideas that they want 
implemented, unusual ascension attempts turn into spectator sports (as 
with DeathOnAStick and his attempt to ascend with scads of diamonds and 
dilithium), and there's generally a very supportive feel about the 
community (one of the first posts I read here was a fellow detailing how 
he'd died with a wand-o-wishing and several other goodies that would be 
free for the taking to the first person to hit level 19 on NAO).

In addition, I'm particularly curious about is this: how have other 
players helped you (or hindered you) in your games?  NetHack isn't a 
multiplayer game (nor will it be, so sayeth the devteam), but between 
the spectator options on NAO and other serverrs, shared bones files, and 
nethack.devnull.net's annual tourney, there's a fair bit of interaction 
(direct or otherwise) between players that I want to discuss.  Certainly 
I've found the game more enjoyable (and have made it much further down) 
since I started visiting here and playing on NAO.


